<sect> ggl_time (Revision 1.1 -- 22/04/98)

<p>The unit <tt/ggl_time/ regroups routines for time conversions of 
different representation. Most of the routines are conversion to or
from <tt/mjd/ (Modified Julian Date), which plays a central role here.
The unit of <tt/mjd/ are days. 

<sect1> Definition of Type ttime

<p> The following time structure is defined:

<tscreen><verb>
struct ttime {
  int year;  
  int month;  
  int day;
  int hour;  
  int min;    
  double sec;
  double mjd;
}
</verb></tscreen>

<p> The following macros are defined:

<tscreen><verb>
define JULDAT0  (2400000.5)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> which allows to convert a mjd (Modified Julian Date) to a Julian Date 
(Julian Date = mjd +JULDAT0)

<tscreen><verb>
SYSMJD0 (40587.0)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> which is the mjd corresponding to the epoch 00.00.00 January 1,1970 UTC.
(Origin of the system time on POSIX systems).

<tscreen><verb>
GPSMJD0  (44244.0)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> which is the mjd corresponding to the epoch 00.00.00 June 6, 1980 UTC.
(Origin of the GPS time system (GPSWEEK =0, SECOFWEEK = 0).


<sect1> Time Conversion Routines
<p>

<sect2> leapyear
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
 leapyear(int year)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Returns <tt/1 (= TRUE)/ if <tt/year/ is a leap year and <tt/0 (=FALSE)/ 
if not.

<sect2>dat_to_mjd
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double dat_to_mjd (const int year, const int month, const int day,
		   const int hour, const int min, const double sec)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of calendar date (<tt/year month day hour min sec/ to <tt/mjd/.
(returned value). 
The calculation  only applies to Gregorian calendar dates. The routine
should not be used for historical dates where the Julian calendar 
was in use (before  1580 ???).

<sect2>datmjd
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int datmjd (struct ttime *t)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Same conversion as with routine <tt/dat_to_mjd/. The fields <tt/year
 month day hour, min sec/ from the struct <tt/ttime/ are used to calculate
mjd, which is stored in the field <tt/mjd/ of the struct.

<sect2>mjd_to_dat
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int mjd_to_dat (double mjd,
		int *year, int *month, int *day,
		int *hour, int *min, double *sec)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of <tt/mjd/ to the corresponding calendar day <tt/year month day
hour min sec/.The calculation  only applies to Gregorian calendar dates. 
The routine should not be used for historical dates where the Julian calendar 
was in use (before  1580 ???).


<sect2>mjddat
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
dint mjddat (struct ttime *t)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Same conversion as with routine <tt/mjd_to_dat/. The fields <tt/mjd/ 
from the struct <tt/ttime/ is used to calculate the calendar date
 which is stored in the field <tt/year month day hour min sec/ of the struct.


<sect2>gps_to_mjd
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double gps_to_mjd (double sec,int week)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of GPS time to <tt/mjd/. The GPS time is given by
the following two values: <tt/week/ which is the GPS week and 
<tt/sec/ which is the second in the GPS week. Note that the order
is first <tt/sec/ than <tt/week/. Start time of GPS time 
<tt/week=0 sec=0/ is epoch 00.00.00 June 6, 1980 UTC


<sect2>mjd_to_gps
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int mjd_to_gps (double mjd, double *sec, int *week)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of <tt/mjd/ to GPS time given by <tt/sec/ (seconds in
GPS week) and <tt/week/ (GPS week).

<sect2>yearday_to_mjd
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double yearday_to_mjd (int year,int daynr)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of <tt/year/ and <tt/daynr/  to mjd (returned value).
 <tt/daynr/ is the day of the years starting with <tt/1/ on January 
1st and ending with <tt/365/ or <tt/366/ on December 31st.

<sect2>mjd_to_yearday
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int mjd_to_yearday (double mjd, int *year, int *daynr)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Conversion of <tt/mjd/ to <tt/year/ and <tt/daynr/. 
<tt/daynr/ is the day of the years starting with <tt/1/ on January 
1st and ending with <tt/365/ or <tt/366/ on December 31st.

<sect2>dayofyear<em/x/
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int dayofyear1 (struct ttime *t)
int dayofyear2 (double mjd)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Both routines return the day of year (1-366). 

<sect2>GMST
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double GMST (double mjd)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Returns the corresponding GMST (Greenwich Mean Sideral Time) for
a given mjd. GMST is returned in <tt/hours/.

<sect2>mjdnow
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double mjdnow()
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Corresponds to the system (HW clock) time, converted to mjd.

<sect2>systimenow
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int systimenow (struct ttime *t)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Corresponds to the system time (HW clock), converted to mjd. Similar to <tt/mjdnow/.
The struct <tt/ttime/ is filled completely (calendar date and mjd).

<sect2>localtimenow
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int localtimenow (struct ttime *t)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Corresponds to the system time (system clock), converted to mjd. Similar to <tt/systimenow/.
In addition, timezone and daylight saving corrections are applied to the system time in
order to obtain the local time.
The struct <tt/ttime/ is filled completely (calendar date and mjd).

<sect1> String Conversion of Time

<sect2>strtime<em/x/
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
char * strtime1 (struct ttime *t, int i)
char * strtime2 (double mjd, int i)
char * strtime3 (struct ttime *t)
</verb></tscreen>
<p><tt/strtime1/ and <tt/strtime2/ return the same string. <tt/strtime1/
takes a struct <tt/ttime/ whereas <tt/strtime2/ takes <tt/mjd/
as input. <tt/i/ is the number of digits of the seconds to be printed.
<p>Example: <tt/strtime2(mjd, 3)/ returns the string 
<tt/"1998 12 31  23 59 30.546"/
<p>The return string of <tt/strime3/ ?????

<sect2>readtime<em/x/
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int    readtime1  (FILE *fpin, struct ttime *t)
double readtime2  (FILE *fpin)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Both routines <tt/readtime1/ and <tt/readtime2/ read from
a file a calendar date of the form <tt/year month day hour min sec/
e.g. (<tt/"1998 12 31  23 59 30.546"/. Years without centuraries 
e.g (<tt/year = 98/) are converted to a full year (<tt/year = 1998/)
<tt/readtime1/ fills the information in a struct <tt/ttime/ adding
mjd. <tt/readtime2/ only returns the corresponding <tt/mjd/.

<sect2>sreadtime<em/x/
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
int    sreadtime1 (const char *instring, struct ttime *t)
double sreadtime2 (const char *instring);
</verb></tscreen>
<p> These routines are similar to the <tt/readtime/ routines. The only
difference is that instead of reading from a file, they take a string
as input parameters.

<sect2>secofday
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
double secofday (const char *mjd)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Some curious routine from E.Favey.




